<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <h2>Tampa Bay fishing through the year</h2>
      <h3>Spring</h3><p>Kingfish move north past the mouth of the bay, with the run peaking in April and May. Snook open for harvest again in March and April.</p>
      <h3>Summer</h3><p>Tarpon roll through the bay from May through August. By late summer, redfish start pushing in from the surrounding waters and the sharks stay busy.</p>
      <h3>Fall</h3><p>Schools of mullet push along the shorelines on their way to the Gulf, and snook, redfish, tarpon and sharks follow them. Kings come back through in October and November.</p>
      <h3>Winter</h3><p>Sheepshead stack up on reefs, rock piles, docks and bridge pilings, and the bottom fishing stays steady nearshore. It's also when the crappie bite on the lakes.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <img src="/images/family-shark-bow.webp" alt="A family on the bow watching a shark come up to the boat" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="1040" style="border-radius:10px;width:100%;height:300px;object-fit:cover">
      <h2 style="margin-top:28px">Your first fishing charter</h2>
      <p>You don't need to know anything before you step on the boat. Your captain rigs the rods, picks the spot and talks you through the bite, the hookset and the fight. Kids are welcome, and inshore and crappie trips are the easiest place for young anglers to start.</p>
      <p>We bring bottled water and ice. You bring food, other drinks, sunglasses, sunblock and clothes for the weather.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

